{
"A later text adds that some of them are like asking how much milk you can get from a cows horn.": "55f07f0b7102",
"He compared the questioner to a man shot with a poisoned arrow who wants to know who shot him before it is removed.": "97264eb85d59",
"He did not leave a blank. In place of those answers he taught suffering, its cause, its end, and the path.": "18967d90cd69",
"His reason: these questions do not lead to peace, insight, and release.": "e5985438b2f0",
"In Pragmatism William James proposed a method for exactly these disputes. The pragmatic method is primarily a method of settling metaphysical disputes that otherwise might be interminable. Is the world one or many?—fated or free?—material or spiritual? The method is to ask, What difference would it practically make to anyone if this notion rather than that notion were true? If no practical difference whatever can be traced, then the alternatives mean practically the same thing, and all dispute is idle. This is the arrow in the language of an American philosopher, and the comparison is often made. There is a difference in what the test finds. For James, a dispute without practical consequences is empty, and the two sides mean practically the same thing. The Buddha did not say that the cosmos is eternal and the cosmos is not eternal mean the same. He said that holding either one keeps a person tangled in views and clinging. James finds the questions idle, and the Buddha finds them harmful.": "34b088e26a8e",
"The Buddha refused to say whether the world is eternal or what happens to an awakened person after death.": "bbd3a3b86b89",
"The Critique of Pure Reason opens, in J. M. D. Meiklejohns translation, with a sentence that could stand beside Māluṅkyaputtas demand. Human reason, in one sphere of its cognition, is called upon to consider questions, which it cannot decline, as they are presented by its own nature, but which it cannot answer, as they transcend every faculty of the mind. Kants list overlaps the Buddhas closely: whether the world has a beginning, whether it is finite, whether the soul is simple and survives. He also diagnosed the cause. Reason rises from principles that work in experience to principles that transcend it, and the arena of these endless contests is called Metaphysic. The lesson on the four corners showed how Kant dissolved these questions. Here the point is his explanation of why people cannot stop asking them. The Buddhas monk was not idle. He was doing what Kant says reason does by its nature. Kants cure was a critique of reason. The Buddhas was to turn attention to the arrow.": "11e2fdd1cd2f",
"The Pali discourses list ten questions the Buddha left undeclared; he has set them aside and turned them down. In Bhikkhu Sujatos translation: the cosmos is eternal, or not eternal, or finite, or infinite; the soul and the body are one and the same, or the soul is one thing, the body another; after death, a realized one still exists, or no longer exists, or both still exists and no longer exists, or neither still exists nor no longer exists (Majjhima Nikāya 63). Later Sanskrit and Chinese works count fourteen by giving each of the first two questions four corners. The Treatise on the Great Perfection of Wisdom lists the fourteen difficult questions and reports a critics inference: since the Buddha did not answer them, he cannot have been omniscient.": "ee5e824dfb92",
"The Tractatus ends with a silence that readers have often compared to the Buddhas. In C. K. Ogdens translation: We feel that even if all possible scientific questions be answered, the problems of life have still not been touched at all. And: The solution of the problem of life is seen in the vanishing of this problem. Then the book calls its own propositions a ladder: He must so to speak throw away the ladder, after he has climbed up on it. And the last line: Whereof one cannot speak, thereof one must be silent. Two things here are close to the Buddha. Answers to theoretical questions leave the problem of life untouched, which is the arrow again, and the deepest questions are resolved when they vanish, not when they are answered. The difference is in the tone of the silence. Wittgensteins silence surrounds what he called the mystical, something that shows itself but cannot be said. The Buddhas silence on the ten questions is practical, a doctors refusal to discuss the archer, and he spoke at length about the path.": "b228a503a186",
"The closest is William James. His test, what practical difference an answer would make, is the arrow turned into a method, and he applied it to nearly the same questions. Kant is closest on why the questions arise and keep arising, since reason asks them by its own nature. Wittgenstein is closest on the relation between theory and life: answer every theoretical question and the problems of life have still not been touched at all.": "239a4358620c",
"The famous answer is a simile. The monk Māluṅkyaputta threatens to leave the order unless the Buddha tells him whether the cosmos is eternal. The Buddha replies: Suppose a man was struck by an arrow thickly smeared with poison. His friends and colleagues, relatives and kin would get a surgeon to treat him. But the man would say: I wont extract this arrow as long as I dont know whether the man who wounded me was an aristocrat, a brahmin, a peasant, or a menial. He goes on to demand the archers name and clan, his height and complexion, his village, and whether the bow was straight or recurved. That man would still not have learned these things, and meanwhile theyd die. The Chinese version lists even more: the wood of the bow, the sinew of its string, the feathers of the arrow, and the smith who forged its head.": "dc8c6022d949",
"The first further step is the diagnosis of why the questions grip us. Kant blamed the nature of reason, and James blamed bad method. The Buddha located the grip in the self. Look at the list again. Is the cosmos eternal? Is the soul the same as the body? What happens to a realized one after death? Each question is, underneath, a question about what will become of me, asked by someone who takes himself to be a lasting self. That is why the answers are not merely idle. They feed the clinging that the arrow stands for. In Majjhima Nikāya 72, when the wanderer Vaccha asks whether the Buddha holds any view at all, the reply is that a realized one has done away with convictions, because he has seen the rise and fall of form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness. When there is no self to protect, the questions lose their urgency.": "f018afae6f92",
"The likeness ends at two points. First, the Buddhas judgment on the questions is stronger than James. They are not merely idle. Each answer is a view that a person holds on to, and holding on to views is one of the four kinds of clinging. The Great Perfection of Wisdom says that answering them brings no benefit and some loss, and leads into wrong views. Second, the Buddhas silence has a definite content beside it. He did not say that nothing can be said, as the end of the Tractatus seems to. He said that these things are undeclared and the four truths are declared, and he asked his students to remember which was which. The silence is a teachers judgment about what to teach, and it is not a mystics judgment about the limits of language.": "0cb8430056b9",
"The second step is that Mahāyāna gave silence a new meaning. In the Vimalakīrti Sūtra, a gathering of bodhisattvas each explain how one enters the Dharma gate of nonduality. Mañjuśrī says that it is beyond words and speech, beyond questions and answers. Then he asks Vimalakīrti, who says nothing. In Kumārajīvas translation, Vimalakīrti was silent and spoke no word. Mañjuśrī praised him: Excellent, excellent! To have no letters and no words at all, this is truly entering the gate of nonduality. This silence is closer to Wittgensteins than the Buddhas silence before Māluṅkyaputta was. It shows what cannot be said. A teacher should keep the two apart. The early silence concerns what not to teach because it does not help, and the later silence concerns what no teaching can contain. Both are Buddhist, and they answer different questions.": "9b771fc7947a",
"Then the Buddha gives his reason, and the reason is the heart of the lesson. And why havent I declared these things? Because they arent beneficial or relevant to the fundamentals of the spiritual life. They dont lead to disillusionment, dispassion, cessation, peace, insight, awakening, and extinguishment. And what has he declared? Suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the path. Those he declared because they do lead there. The Great Perfection of Wisdom adds a second reason. Because these things have no reality, he does not answer. It is like asking how many pints of milk can be got by milking a cows horn: this is not a real question, and should not be answered. One reason is practical and the other concerns the question itself. The Buddhas silence has both.": "3e6e59224097",
"Westerners often take the Buddhas silence in one of two wrong ways. Some think he was hiding a secret answer, usually that the self survives. Others think he was saying the questions are meaningless. Begin with the arrow, which everyone understands at once, and ask the audience what questions they would want answered before letting the surgeon work. Then give the Buddhas reason in his own words: these questions do not lead to peace, insight, and release. Add the cows horn from the Great Perfection of Wisdom, to show that some of the questions are also badly formed. William James question, what practical difference would it make, is a good bridge for an American audience. End by saying what the Buddha did teach, because the silence makes sense only beside it.": "4169cbe5967a",
"William James asked the same thing of metaphysics: what practical difference would the answer make?": "2c6b3534e020"
}
